**: The residuals should be independent of each other under the null hypothesis.\n3. **Homoscedasticity**: The variance of the residuals should be constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. **Normality**: The residuals should be normally distributed, particularly in small samples.\n\nThese assumptions are critical because violations can lead to misleading test results. For instance, if the residuals are heteroscedastic, the test may indicate the presence of autocorrelation when it is not present, or vice versa.\n\n**Interpretation**  \nUpon conducting the Godfrey test, if the calculated LM statistic exceeds the critical value from the chi-squared distribution at a specified significance level (e.g., 0.05), the null hypothesis of no autocorrelation is rejected. This indicates that autocorrelation is present in the residuals, prompting the researcher to consider alternative modeling strategies, such as including lagged dependent variables or using generalized least squares (GLS) to correct for the autocorrelation.\n\nIn summary, the Godfrey test is a vital tool in econometrics for diagnosing autocorrelation in regression models. By ensuring that the assumptions of the test are met, researchers can effectively utilize this method to validate their models and enhance the reliability of their econometric analyses.", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}], "created": 1771166015, "model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_f97eff32c5", "usage": {"com